      "The Central Highlands are built of ancient metamorphic rocks, mainly metasediments and the charnockitic gneiss common on the island, which have been folded and faulted several times. Long weathering and erosion are estimated to have stripped away more than 3,000 m of rock, and the debris concentrated in the valleys as gem-bearing gravels. In outline the massif is roughly anchor-shaped: a central ridge running roughly north–south, whose southern end spreads east and west into a curved mountain wall.",
